    bottomLine:
      "The electric pressure washer worth paying for. The induction motor is the whole reason, and it will outlast three cheap machines.",
    description:
      "Almost every complaint about electric pressure washers, that they are loud, that they get hot, that they die after two summers, traces to the universal motor most of them use. The RY142300 uses a brushless induction motor instead, and the difference is immediately obvious. It was the quietest machine in our entire test group, gas or electric, running at a level where conversation nearby is easy. Over extended sessions it showed none of the power fade that heat-soaked universal motors produce, and the housing stayed cool enough to touch. At 2300 PSI it sits at the higher end of the electric range, and in our testing it cleaned vinyl siding, a weathered wood deck, a cedar fence, and patio furniture without difficulty. On concrete it works, though the 1.2 GPM water volume means a driveway takes real time, and a surface cleaner attachment is close to mandatory if you plan on that job regularly. The on-board storage is a genuinely useful design touch, keeping the hose, cord, wand, and all five nozzles on the unit rather than scattered across a garage shelf. The trade-offs are throughput and price. Water volume is the specification electric machines cannot escape, and 1.2 GPM is modest even for the category. It also costs meaningfully more than budget electric units, which is exactly the point: you are paying for a motor that lasts.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-gpw3000-electric",
    name: "Greenworks Pro GPW3000 3000 PSI",
    category: "3000 PSI, 2.0 GPM, Induction Motor, Steel Frame",
    badge: "Most Powerful",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "3000 PSI at 2.0 GPM gives 6,000 cleaning units, which genuinely cleans concrete",
