    bottomLine:
      "Cheap, popular, and genuinely functional. Buy it knowing it is a few-seasons machine rather than a decade-long one.",
    description:
      "The SPX3000 has been among the best-selling pressure washers in the country for years, and the reasons are straightforward: it costs a fraction of a gas machine, it produces enough pressure and water volume to clean siding, decks, fences, furniture, and vehicles effectively, and it is small enough to store anywhere. For a homeowner who pressure washes a handful of times a year, that covers the real demand at minimal cost. Its dual detergent tanks are genuinely unusual at this price. Keeping a house wash in one tank and a degreaser in the other, and switching between them with a dial rather than draining and rinsing, is more convenient than most machines costing several times as much. The Total Stop System is the other important feature, cutting the pump when the trigger releases, which prevents the heat buildup that destroys pumps left running in bypass. The honest limitation is the motor. This uses a universal motor, which is why it is loud, why it heats up during long sessions with noticeable power fade, and why its expected service life is measured in a few seasons of regular use rather than a decade. The plastic frame and wheels reflect the same economics. Framed as an inexpensive tool with a finite life, it is a reasonable purchase. Framed as a long-term investment, it is not.",
