      "Most pressure washer damage happens because the operator applied more pressure than the surface could tolerate, and Karcher Smart Control addresses that directly. You choose the surface, siding, wood, car, or masonry, and the machine sets an appropriate pressure automatically and displays it on the wand. In our testing it was the machine least likely to cause accidental damage, and the one we would hand to someone who has never used a pressure washer before without a long safety briefing. For vehicles it is the standout in this guide. Its foam cannon produces thick, clinging foam that dwells on paint long enough to lift road film before rinsing, and its available pressure levels are appropriate for automotive paint rather than dangerous to it. The water-cooled induction motor keeps noise low and expected service life high, and the integrated hose reel solves the perennial problem of a stiff high-pressure hose ending up in a tangled pile. Two limitations. Its 2000 PSI at 1.4 GPM produces 2,800 cleaning units, which is well below what makes a driveway practical, so this is a machine for vertical surfaces and vehicles rather than large concrete. And Karcher uses a proprietary accessory fitting rather than the standard quick-connect that most machines share, which means aftermarket nozzles and surface cleaners either need an adapter or are unavailable.",
