        <!-- SECTION 4: Water Supply, Cavitation, and Why Pumps Fail -->
        <div id="water-supply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Water Supply, Cavitation, and Why Pumps Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric pressure washer pumps almost always fail for reasons unrelated to the electrical side, and understanding the water side prevents nearly all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The pump must never be starved of water.</strong> A pressure washer pump is designed to move a specific volume, and if the supply cannot deliver it, the pump draws air along with water. That produces cavitation, tiny vapor bubbles collapsing violently inside the pump, which erodes seals and valves rapidly. A machine that stutters, pulses, or produces inconsistent pressure is usually cavitating rather than failing electrically.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a short, wide garden hose from a fully open spigot.</strong> A 5/8-inch or 3/4-inch hose no longer than about 25 feet, connected to a spigot opened all the way, gives the pump the supply it needs. A long, narrow, or kinked hose is the most common cause of supply starvation, and it is completely invisible as a problem until the pump fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Turn the water on before starting the machine.</strong> Starting a pump dry, even briefly, damages seals. Connect the hose, open the spigot fully, squeeze the trigger to purge air from the system until water flows steadily, and only then switch the machine on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run in bypass.</strong> With the machine on and the trigger released, water circulates inside the pump and heats up quickly. Sustained bypass is the leading cause of pump failure across all pressure washers. A Total Stop System shuts the machine off automatically when you release the trigger, which is why it is a feature worth insisting on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the inlet screen.</strong> A small filter at the water inlet catches grit that would otherwise reach the pump, and it clogs gradually. A partially blocked screen starves the pump with no symptom other than reduced pressure that people usually blame on the machine wearing out. Pull it and rinse it a few times each season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Circuit Nobody Checks</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Higher-output electric pressure washers draw enough current to trip a shared 15-amp circuit, particularly at startup. Running one on a circuit that also serves a refrigerator or a garage freezer is a recipe for nuisance trips and, worse, for a freezer quietly thawing. Give a powerful electric washer its own 20-amp circuit, and if you must use an extension cord, use a heavy-gauge outdoor-rated one as short as possible.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Nozzles, Detergents, and Attachments -->
        <div id="nozzles-and-accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Nozzles, Detergents, and Attachments
          </h2>

          <p>
            On an electric machine, where you have less raw power to spare, choosing the right nozzle and using detergent properly matter more than they do on a high-output gas unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Nozzle Colors</h3>

          <p>
            Red is 0 degrees and concentrates everything into a pencil jet that damages almost any surface. Yellow is 15 degrees for aggressive work on hard surfaces. Green is 25 degrees, the general-purpose choice. White is 40 degrees for siding, vehicles, and delicate materials. Black is a low-pressure soap nozzle, and it is the only one that will draw detergent through the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Detergent Only Works at Low Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            The black soap nozzle exists because the detergent siphon only engages at low pressure. Trying to apply soap through a high-pressure tip simply will not draw it, which is the most common reason people conclude their detergent system is broken. Apply soap with the black tip, let it dwell for several minutes without drying, then switch to a rinsing tip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let Chemistry Do the Work</h3>

          <p>
            This matters more on electric machines than gas ones. A proper cleaner that dwells on the surface breaks down algae, mildew, and road film chemically, so you need far less pressure to remove it. On a deck or siding, a dedicated cleaner applied first turns a marginal electric machine into an entirely adequate one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surface Cleaners Close the Concrete Gap</h3>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner is a shrouded disc with rotating jets that you push across flat concrete. It roughly doubles cleaning speed and eliminates the striping that hand-waving a wand always produces. Check that your machine has enough output to spin one, since some require a minimum GPM, and confirm the fitting matches your machine rather than being proprietary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Wands Beat Ladders</h3>

          <p>
            A telescoping wand lets you reach second-story siding and soffits from the ground. Using a pressure washer from a ladder is genuinely dangerous because of recoil, and an inexpensive extension eliminates that risk entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Storage, Freeze Protection, and Longevity -->
        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Storage, Freeze Protection, and Longevity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric pressure washers have no engine to winterize, which leads people to assume no winter preparation is needed. The pump still needs protecting, and freezing is the most common way these machines die.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water left in the pump will crack it.</strong> Water expands as it freezes, and a consumer pump housing is not designed to survive that. A cracked pump is a terminal failure on most electric machines because the pump is not economically replaceable. This is the single most common cause of an electric pressure washer that worked fine in October and does nothing in April.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run pump antifreeze through it.</strong> After the last use of the season, disconnect the water supply and draw pump saver antifreeze through the machine until it comes out of the wand. This displaces the water and lubricates the seals, which also prevents them from drying out and hardening over months of storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it above freezing if you can.</strong> A heated basement or a conditioned space removes the risk entirely. An unheated garage in a cold climate does not, even if it rarely freezes hard, because a single overnight freeze is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coil the high-pressure hose in large loops.</strong> High-pressure hose is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tightly or crushed under stored items. A kinked hose can burst under pressure, which is both dangerous and an expensive replacement. Hang it in wide loops rather than piling it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store the wand and nozzles together.</strong> Losing the black soap nozzle is remarkably common, and it is the one you cannot substitute for, since it is the only tip that lets the detergent siphon engage. Machines with on-board nozzle storage largely solve this; if yours does not, a small box on a shelf works.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the cord before each season.</strong> Pressure washers involve water and electricity in close proximity, and a nicked or cracked power cord is a genuine hazard. Inspect the full length, and always plug into a GFCI-protected outlet, which most machines require anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

