        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Electric Pressure Washer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric machines fail in predictable ways, and none of the causes are the electrical side that people worry about.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Motor Type Entirely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two machines with identical PSI figures can have completely different service lives depending on whether the motor is induction or universal. Listings often omit it, and a very light machine with high claimed pressure is almost certainly universal. Paying more for induction is the single best value decision in this category.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starving the Pump With a Long, Narrow Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pressure washer pump must receive its full rated volume, and a long, narrow, or kinked garden hose cannot supply it. The pump then draws air, cavitates, and erodes its own seals. Use a 5/8-inch or larger hose no longer than about 25 feet from a fully open spigot, and clean the inlet screen regularly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Apply Soap Through a High-Pressure Nozzle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The detergent siphon only engages at low pressure, which is why the black soap nozzle exists. Using any other tip means no detergent will be drawn through, and people routinely conclude the soap system is broken. Apply with the black tip, let it dwell without drying, then switch tips to rinse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Winterization Because There Is No Engine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The absence of an engine leads people to store electric machines without any preparation, but the pump still holds water, and one freeze cracks the housing permanently. Run pump antifreeze through it at the end of the season and store it somewhere above freezing. It takes five minutes and saves the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

